{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/app at exact clean head 1be57f9ce08972a8d94f3ba9c053513ca5b61836, succeeding 1bb07664. Companion to the internal/workroom artifact 0b80576b and the docs artifact at this same head; all three must be co-signed together. Answers request e8adc06c under promise 0fb763a0.\n\nWHAT CHANGED HERE: one comment, and it was false. RetireActor's doc comment said that after retirement \"what stops it acting again is the removal of its key from local custody, because the durable log admits any allowlisted signer.\" That was an accurate description of the system before this head and is wrong after it. The fold now refuses a state or ratification from a non-participant however the record reaches the log, so key deletion is hygiene rather than the boundary.\n\nWHY A COMMENT IS WORTH AN ARTIFACT. A comment that names the wrong layer is worse than no comment: it sends the next reader looking for the guard in local custody, finds nothing enforcing anything there, and either concludes the boundary does not exist or reimplements it in the wrong place. This one sat directly above the function an operator calls to remove someone, which is exactly where a reader goes to ask \"what does removal actually do\". It now says the fold is the boundary, and that deleting the key still matters for a different reason — leaving key files behind enlarges the shared key directory with principals nobody is watching.\n\nNO BEHAVIOUR CHANGES AT THIS PATH. RetireActor still supersedes the membership statement and still deletes the key file; the sequence, the records it appends and its error handling are untouched. The security property it describes is now enforced a layer down, in internal/workroom, and the companion artifact there carries that change and the measurement behind it.\n\nSECURITY: this path gets strictly stronger without moving. Before, an operator who ran RetireActor and whose key deletion silently failed — or who removed a principal that held a key in another clone — had no durable boundary at all. Now the removal is enforced by the fold regardless of what happened to the key material, and local custody deletion is defence in depth rather than the only defence.\n\nGATES at this exact head on a clean tree: go vet ./... exit 0; go build ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok, internal/app among them; go test -race ./... -count=1 exit 0; gofmt -l . empty; git diff --check clean. Rebased onto current main 7a02f3e1 after codex's docs re-anchoring landed, with three-dot and two-dot diffs identical, so the rebase reverted nothing.","body":{"commit":"1be57f9ce08972a8d94f3ba9c053513ca5b61836","path":"internal/app"}}